      "input": {
        "question_id": "8911_8353800871456",
        "question": "Based on Figure 2, which statement most accurately describes the relationship between the pneumomediastinum and pneumopericardium, and what clinical insight does this provide regarding the patient's condition?\n\nA) The pneumomediastinum is localized only to the lower mediastinum, while the pneumopericardium is extensive, indicating a high likelihood of cardiac tamponade.\nB) The pneumomediastinum extends into the neck, while the pneumopericardium is confined to the cardiac silhouette, suggesting a severe rupture of the oesophagus with potential for mediastinitis.\nC) Both pneumomediastinum and pneumopericardium are localized to the upper chest, indicating a minor rupture with limited risk of further complications.\nD) The pneumomediastinum surrounds the heart and extends superiorly, while the pneumopericardium is minimal, suggesting the primary source of air is distal oesophageal perforation.\nE) The pneumopericardium is extensive and overshadows the pneumomediastinum, indicating the air source is likely cardiac trauma rather than oesophageal perforation.\nF) The pneumomediastinum is more pronounced than the pneumopericardium, extending into the neck, indicating significant oesophageal perforation consistent with Boerhaave's syndrome.",
        "images": [
          "figures/8911/figure_2.jpg"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
